WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Speaker 1: All right, let's jump jump right&nbsp;
into this topic, it's a big topic, it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:09.760
complicated one. Over the last several weeks,&nbsp;
I've gotten a ton of emails from you guys, calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.800
with people asking or suggesting maybe if&nbsp;
you are unvaccinated and you get very sick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.200
with covid and you go to the hospital, you&nbsp;
should be refused treatment. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:27.120
that's not going to happen in any real way.&nbsp;
There are primary care doctors that are saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.240
our practice will only treat what will only have&nbsp;
on a roster of vaccinated patients. But if you go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.040
to an emergency room, you are not going to be&nbsp;
denied treatment because you are unvaccinated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.240
for better or worse, ethical, unethical. That's&nbsp;
just not going to happen. But there are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.840
new questions. It's a much more narrow discussion.&nbsp;
And the discussion is whether it would be fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:57.200
to treat vaccinated covid patients first. And&nbsp;
this is more of a triage conversation. So really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.400
it's a question of whether vaccination status&nbsp;
should or could be considered in terms of triage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.440
And there's some pretty good arguments to suggest&nbsp;
that it should, which we are going to get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.840
interesting Wired article, which asks, would it&nbsp;
be fair to treat vaccinated covid patients first?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:18.400
Last week, tuks Texas health care policymakers&nbsp;
discussed taking vaccination status into account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:24.880
for covid triage. It's a larger conversation&nbsp;
ethicists are bracing for. It's no accident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.400
that people describe something that's calculated,&nbsp;
cold and unemotional as clinical. Taking care of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.800
people's health isn't health isn't emotion free by&nbsp;
any means. But the clinic is where physicians and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.080
nurses have to make life and death decisions under&nbsp;
time pressure, including which patients go first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:48.080
and what kind of care they get when resources&nbsp;
like, say, ventilators or intensive care beds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.920
oxygen staff and time are scarce. That decision&nbsp;
making is called triage. And last week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:59.360
the Dallas Morning News reported that some Texas&nbsp;
hospitals were considering a massive change to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.640
how they do it. The question in play, would it&nbsp;
make sense to take into account the vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:11.360
status of their covid patients with ICU filling up&nbsp;
with severely ill covid patients and a shortage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:17.120
beds and nurses should clinicians all other things&nbsp;
being equal? That's an important part of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:24.800
Care for vaccinated people before or differently&nbsp;
than unvaccinated ones. And the article goes on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:32.560
to say it hasn't happened and probably won't.&nbsp;
The idea came from a memo. This is this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:40.560
very complicated question. Fundamentally, this is&nbsp;
not about ignoring the Hippocratic Oath and saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:47.040
don't treat unvaccinated patients. They haven't&nbsp;
taken care of themselves, assuming there's no real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.680
medical reason why they're unvaccinated. We're&nbsp;
going to ignore these people, that that's not what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:03:00.320
this is about. This is about including vaccination&nbsp;
status as part of triage, where someone may end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:07.280
getting less care or might end up getting no care,&nbsp;
depending on how full an emergency room is. In a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:14.160
moment, I'll tell you about three emergency rooms&nbsp;
that are so full they've closed and whether the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:21.840
vaccination status affects chance of recovery. And&nbsp;
you could on this basis say, all right, you've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:31.680
a 65 year old vaccinated patient and a 60 year old&nbsp;
unvaccinated patient. And if you don't consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:38.640
vaccination status, you might just go by how&nbsp;
severe are their symptoms, their age, etc..&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.880
If you consider vaccination status, you would have&nbsp;
this statistical framework in which you know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.760
hospitalized, vaccinated patients are more likely&nbsp;
to survive than unvaccinated patients. That would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:56.000
be a way to empirically, not in some kind of&nbsp;
moralistic punishment way, but empirically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:03.360
consider vaccination status in triage. Is a&nbsp;
vaccinated 50 year old more likely to recover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:11.040
than an unvaccinated 40 year old? And there's even&nbsp;
another aspect of this, which is if you have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:17.920
patient in the E.R. or in the ICU or potentially&nbsp;
going into the ICU and they're unvaccinated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:22.960
and they're unvaccinated for no other reason&nbsp;
other than they fell for conspiracy theories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:30.240
does it stand to reason that they may generally be&nbsp;
less likely to heed the advice that they are given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:36.400
in that hospital if granted an intensive care&nbsp;
unit? Would it be fair to consider that as well?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.920
And of course, there's there's the&nbsp;
important inclusion of the idea, all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:47.920
else being equal. And really the I think the gist&nbsp;
here is not to make unclear triage calls about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.280
a vaccinated 55 year old and an unvaccinated&nbsp;
40 year old, but really more about situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:05:00.720
where it's almost identical situations. You&nbsp;
might have two people equally sick. Same age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:07.040
one vaccinated, one unvaccinated, and you have&nbsp;
one spot in the ICU, would it be fair to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:11.600
we're going to take the vaccinated patient because&nbsp;
statistically they're more likely to recover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:18.800
and in general, they are probably more likely to&nbsp;
heed our advice. This is not unheard of elsewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:28.000
on the basis of a different sort of sort of health&nbsp;
considerations. Alcoholics historically have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.800
considered unsuitable for liver transplants&nbsp;
because of the presumed high risk of relapsing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.640
to excessive drinking, which could ruin the&nbsp;
transplanted liver. That's that's a lifestyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.120
choice that is factored into organ donation,&nbsp;
although there are people who rightly say, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.000
it's not really a lifestyle choice. Alcoholism is&nbsp;
a disease and then it becomes more complex. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.440
the point is that it is it's similar. It's&nbsp;
analogous to some degree. Triage is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.480
chances of survival, but it's also about available&nbsp;
resources. It's also about patient willingness to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.280
comply with medical advice. And people who are&nbsp;
vaccinated are not only more likely to survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:12.880
they've shown a motivation to follow the&nbsp;
widely accepted medical advice. And ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.920
this is about available resources. And we've&nbsp;
talked before and I'm going to give you an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:25.280
in the next story of this. When you have a full&nbsp;
ICU with disproportionately unvaccinated people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.520
not because of a medical condition that says don't&nbsp;
get vaccinated, but because they've chosen not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.880
get vaccinated. If you get into a car accident,&nbsp;
if you have a heart attack, if you have a work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:41.040
injury and you're vaccinated and you go in for&nbsp;
something serious, not related to covid, you may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.240
not be able to get the treatment you need because&nbsp;
of people who have simply chosen not to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:51.760
vaccinated. And so there is an element of this,&nbsp;
which is that people who are not looking out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:58.320
society are consuming resources and hurting other&nbsp;
people, people who maybe would survive after that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:03.680
car crash if they could get right into an ICU. But&nbsp;
the ICU is full because of unvaccinated people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:08.000
So I think it's completely reasonable to take it&nbsp;
into account in triage. I'm not saying you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.400
provide treatment, but the question about triage&nbsp;
considerations seems completely logical to me.

